# November 21, 2026: Kansas, a first statewide scatter. Kansas had 19 places, all round Kansas City, Lawrence and
# Topeka; this batch spreads 17 across the rest of the state: the frontier forts (Larned, Scott, Hays, Harker), the
# Hollenberg Pony Express station, Nicodemus, the Flint Hills (Chase County Courthouse, Spring Hill Ranch, Kaw Mission),
# the Beecher Bible and Rifle Church, the Cathedral of the Plains, Big Brutus, Coronado Heights, the Garden of Eden,
# the Mine Creek battlefield, Cottonwood Ranch and the Goodnow House.
# Facts from the linked sources only: Wikipedia read with tools/wiki_raw.py, the National Park Service's and the Kansas
# Historical Society's own pages (hours, addresses, Kansapedia), and the managing bodies' pages read with tools/page.py.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
KS='https://www.kansashistory.gov/'
BATCH={'key':'ks_nrqf','label':'Kansas: a first statewide scatter','added':'2026-11-21 14:26'}
PLACES=[
N('fort-larned-ks','Fort Larned','Larned, Kansas','ks','fort','standing',38.18306,-99.21806,'site','open','walk','forts','1859–1878',
  'The Army set up a camp on the Pawnee Fork in October 1859 to guard traffic on the Santa Fe Trail, moved it upstream in 1860 and named it Fort Larned. It also served as an agency for the Plains tribes under the 1861 Fort Wise treaty. The first sod and adobe buildings were replaced by sandstone ones between 1866 and 1868, and the post closed in 1878 once the tribes had been moved to reservations and the railroads had ended the trail.',
  'Nine sandstone buildings round the parade ground: barracks, the old commissary and quartermaster storehouse with their rifle loopholes, officers\' quarters and the shops. Most are furnished as they were. The blockhouse is the one building rebuilt entirely, on its original foundation (Wikipedia). The Park Service counts it among the best-preserved forts of the Indian Wars period.',
  'A whole sandstone fort of the Santa Fe Trail days, still standing round its parade ground.',
  'Fort Larned National Historic Site, National Park Service, about 5.5 miles west of Larned on KS 156. The Park Service\'s hours page says the visitor center and grounds are open 8:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. and the parking-lot gate is locked at 5 p.m.; it may close or open late in heavy snow or ice.',
  [W('Fort Larned National Historic Site'),['NPS: Fort Larned hours','https://www.nps.gov/fols/planyourvisit/hours.htm']],
  th=[38.18236,-99.22142],thn='Fort Larned parking lot',brick='no',hours='Daily 8:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. (visitor center and grounds)'),
N('fort-scott-ks','Fort Scott','Fort Scott, Kansas','ks','fort','standing',37.8434,-94.70449,'site','open','walk','forts','1842–1873',
  'The Army founded Fort Scott in 1842 on the military road between Fort Leavenworth and Fort Gibson, on what was then the edge of settlement; its busiest years were 1842 to 1853. It was used again during Bleeding Kansas and the Civil War, and the troops left for good in 1873. The buildings stood empty and decayed until 1965, when the Park Service helped the city restore them.',
  'Twenty historic structures round the parade ground in the middle of the town: officers\' quarters, dragoon and infantry barracks, a hospital, guardhouse, stables, post headquarters, bake shop and powder magazine, with five acres of restored tallgrass prairie (Wikipedia). Which buildings are original and which rebuilt was not checked.',
  'A frontier fort of the 1840s restored in the middle of the town that grew round it.',
  'Fort Scott National Historic Site, National Park Service. The visitor center page gives the buildings\' hours; a 2024 notice says they close on Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year\'s Day, and that the grounds, walks and lot open from half an hour before sunrise to half an hour after sunset. Park in the lot on East Wall Street.',
  [W('Fort Scott National Historic Site'),['NPS: Fort Scott visitor center','https://www.nps.gov/fosc/planyourvisit/visitorcenters.htm'],['NPS: winter hours notice, 2024','https://www.nps.gov/fosc/learn/news/winter-hours-of-operation-2024.htm']],
  th=[37.84267,-94.70379],thn='Lot on East Wall Street',brick='some',hours='April to October daily 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.; November to March 8:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.'),
N('fort-hays-ks','Fort Hays','Hays, Kansas','ks','fort','standing',38.86167,-99.34222,'site','open','walk','forts','1865–1889',
  'The Army set up Fort Fletcher on the Smoky Hill Trail in October 1865 to guard the Butterfield Overland Despatch, closed it in 1866 and reopened it a mile north to guard the men building the Union Pacific Eastern Division; it was renamed Fort Hays. There was no wall round the post, only a blockhouse. It closed in 1889 and became a historical park in 1929.',
  'Four of the fort\'s own buildings round the parade ground: the 1867 blockhouse and 1872 guardhouse, both with exhibits, and two officers\' quarters furnished as in the 1860s to 1880s, with a 1967 visitor center (Wikipedia).',
  'A blockhouse instead of a stockade: the one defence of a fort on the railroad.',
  'Fort Hays State Historic Site, Kansas Historical Society, 1472 US 183 Alternate, four miles south of I-70 exit 157. Hours from the society\'s page; closed state holidays.',
  [W('Fort Hays'),['Kansas Historical Society: Fort Hays',KS+'fort_hays']],
  brick='?',hours='Wednesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., Sunday 1 to 5 p.m.; closed state holidays'),
N('hollenberg-pony-express-station','Hollenberg Pony Express Station','Hanover, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.90083,-96.84361,'site','open','drive','','1857–1861',
  'Gerat Hollenberg built this frame way station in 1857 or 1858 for travellers on the Oregon and California trails. It became a home station of the Pony Express, where riders changed mounts, in 1860, and served the Butterfield Overland Mail too; the Pony Express lasted only 18 months. After Hollenberg died in 1874 it was a farmstead until 1941. It is counted the most intact surviving Pony Express station and is a National Historic Landmark.',
  'A long story-and-a-half building of cottonwood frame with a shingled gable roof on a stone foundation: six rooms (family rooms, a shop and a tavern room) and the loft where riders slept (Wikipedia). Interior not checked.',
  'The best-kept station left on the whole Pony Express route.',
  'Owned by the state; Hollenberg Pony Express Station State Historic Site, Kansas Historical Society, 2889 23rd Road, one mile east of Hanover on K-243. In November 2026 the society\'s page says "closed until further notice" and also gives the usual season: Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. and Sunday 1 to 5 p.m., closing October 4, 2026 and opening again in April 2027. Check before going.',
  [W('Hollenberg Pony Express Station'),['Kansas Historical Society: Hollenberg station',KS+'hollenberg']],
  th=[39.90152,-96.84689],thn='Visitor center lot, 23rd Road',brick='no',hours='Closed until further notice (November 2026); usual season April to early October, Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., Sunday 1 to 5'),
N('nicodemus-ks','Nicodemus','Nicodemus, Kansas','ks','town','standing',39.39083,-99.6175,'area','open','walk','','1877',
  'Nicodemus was founded in 1877 by a town company of six Black men and a white land developer, who recruited settlers from churches in Kentucky. By the mid-1880s it had two newspapers, three general stores, at least three churches, hotels, a bank and a livery. It is the only town founded by African Americans in the West during Reconstruction that is still there, and a National Historic Site preserves five of its buildings.',
  'Five buildings of the National Historic Site: the Township Hall (the visitor center), the African Methodist Episcopal Church, the First Baptist Church, the St. Francis Hotel and the District No. 1 schoolhouse (Wikipedia). Which of them can be entered beyond the hall was not checked.',
  'The last town left of those founded in the West by freed people after the Civil War.',
  'Nicodemus National Historic Site, National Park Service, shared with Nicodemus Township, which owns the hall (Park Service page). The Township Hall has exhibits, films and a book store. The pin is the middle of the town (area); park on the streets.',
  [W('Nicodemus National Historic Site'),['NPS: Township Hall','https://home.nps.gov/places/township-hall.htm']],
  brick='?',hours='Township Hall 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., closed Tuesday and Wednesday'),
N('basilica-st-fidelis-victoria','Basilica of St. Fidelis (Cathedral of the Plains)','Victoria, Kansas','ks','worship','standing',38.85646,-99.1506,'exact','open','drive','','1908–1911',
  'Built from 1908 to 1911 by the Volga German parish of Victoria to designs by John T. Comès of Pittsburgh: every member aged 12 or more was asked for $45 and six wagon loads of fencepost limestone and four of sand a year, and some families hauled 70 or 80 loads. William Jennings Bryan called it the Cathedral of the Plains in 1912. It is not a cathedral, but became a minor basilica in 2014, the only one in Kansas.',
  'Romanesque church of local limestone with twin towers 141 feet tall, seen from I-70. Inside, the 48 stained-glass windows made by the Munich Studio of Chicago and put in in 1916 (Wikipedia). A friary of the Capuchins stands beside it.',
  'A Kansas farm parish hauled the stone for a cathedral by wagon.',
  'An active Catholic parish of the Diocese of Salina, 900 Cathedral Avenue. The parish page invites visitors to the Masses and offers tours by arrangement ("Schedule a Tour"). Park on the street.',
  [W('Basilica of St. Fidelis'),['Basilica of St. Fidelis parish','https://stfidelischurch.com/']],
  brick='no',hours='Mass Monday 7 p.m., Tuesday to Friday 6:45 a.m., Saturday 5 p.m., Sunday 10 a.m.; tours by arrangement'),
N('chase-county-courthouse-ks','Chase County Courthouse','Cottonwood Falls, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.37021,-96.54164,'exact','open','walk','','1873',
  'Finished in 1873 to designs by John G. Haskell, one of the architects of the Kansas Capitol, it is the oldest courthouse in Kansas still in use. It is built of limestone quarried on the town site and has a three-story spiral staircase of walnut from the Cottonwood River.',
  'A French Renaissance (Second Empire) courthouse of limestone with a red mansard roof, 113 feet tall, at the south end of Broadway; inside, the walnut spiral staircase (city page). Self-guided tours.',
  'The oldest working courthouse in Kansas, with a walnut staircase three floors high.',
  'County courthouse, still in use. The City of Cottonwood Falls\' page says self-guided tours are open during business hours and weekend or holiday visits are arranged through the Chase County Chamber of Commerce. Park on Broadway.',
  [W('Chase County Courthouse (Kansas)'),['City of Cottonwood Falls: the courthouse','https://cwfks.org/?p=125']],
  brick='no',hours='Monday to Friday 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. (self-guided); weekends and holidays by appointment with the chamber'),
N('spring-hill-ranch-tallgrass','Spring Hill Ranch (Tallgrass Prairie National Preserve)','Strong City, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.43278,-96.55889,'site','open','walk','','1881',
  'Stephen F. Jones, a cattleman from Tennessee, finished this ranch headquarters in 1881, a Second Empire house that cost about $25,000, and was early in the Flint Hills to fence his cattle in with stone walls and wire. He also built the Lower Fox Creek School on land he gave. He sold in 1888 to Barney Lantry. The ranch is now the heart of the Tallgrass Prairie National Preserve.',
  'The limestone ranch house, the three-level limestone barn of 1882 (it shows the orientation film) and the other ranch buildings, open for self-guided tours; the one-room Lower Fox Creek School is nearby. Only the house is heated; the others are open-air as in the 1880s (Park Service).',
  'A cattle baron\'s stone mansion and barn in the last big stretch of tallgrass prairie.',
  'Tallgrass Prairie National Preserve, National Park Service with The Nature Conservancy, on KS 177 north of Strong City. The Park Service says the visitor center and historic buildings open daily except major holidays; a 2024 notice gives winter hours of 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. for the ranch buildings, the visitor center shut Monday and Tuesday from mid-November to mid-April. Trails open 24 hours; fire and cattle or bison work may close them. Lower Fox Creek School: 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., May to December.',
  [W('Tallgrass Prairie National Preserve'),['NPS: hours','https://www.nps.gov/tapr/planyourvisit/hours.htm'],['NPS: basic information','https://www.nps.gov/tapr/planyourvisit/basicinfo.htm'],['NPS: winter notice, 2024','https://www.nps.gov/tapr/learn/news/visitor-center-closed-on-mondays-and-tuesdays-during-winter-months.htm']],
  th=[38.43195,-96.55784],thn='Visitor center lot, KS 177',brick='no',hours='Ranch buildings daily, about 9 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. in winter (2024 notice); closed major holidays'),
N('kaw-mission-council-grove','Kaw Mission','Council Grove, Kansas','ks','civic','standing',38.66594,-96.494,'exact','open','walk','','1851–1854',
  'The Methodist Episcopal Church South built this stone mission and school for the Kaw (Kansa) people, who gave Kansas its name; it was done by February 1851. Thirty Kaw boys lived and studied here under the teacher Thomas Huffaker until 1854, when the fees closed the school. The Kaw were later removed to Indian Territory.',
  'A two-story native stone building of eight rooms, with displays of Kaw objects and frontier furnishings (Wikipedia).',
  'The school house of the people Kansas is named after.',
  'Kaw Mission State Historic Site, Kansas Historical Society, 500 North Mission Street, Council Grove. Hours from the society\'s page.',
  [W('Kaw Mission'),['Kansas Historical Society: Kaw Mission',KS+'kaw_mission']],
  th=[38.66541,-96.49355],thn='Lot on North Mission Street',brick='no',hours='Wednesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., Sunday 1 to 5 p.m.'),
N('beecher-bible-rifle-church','Beecher Bible and Rifle Church','Wabaunsee, Kansas','ks','worship','standing',39.14389,-96.34584,'exact','view','drive','','1862',
  'About 70 New Haven, Connecticut emigrants founded Wabaunsee in 1855, stirred by a sermon of Henry Ward Beecher, who helped pay for rifles that were shipped past proslavery country in crates marked Bibles. The colony\'s church took four years to build and was finished in 1862.',
  'A plain church of local limestone with contrasting stone trim, built by the member Robert Banks (Wikipedia), at the corner of Chapel and Elm streets. Interior not checked.',
  'Named for the rifles shipped to Kansas as "Beecher\'s Bibles".',
  'An active church. The Wamego visitors\' page says services are still held on Sundays and tours are given on request; no page of the congregation\'s own was found, so no times are given. Look from the street otherwise.',
  [W('Beecher Bible and Rifle Church'),['Visit Wamego: the church','https://old-site.visitwamego.com/see-and-do/beecher-bible-rifle-church']],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('big-brutus-west-mineral','Big Brutus','West Mineral, Kansas','ks','industry','standing',37.27353,-94.93823,'exact','open','walk','','1962–1974',
  'Big Brutus is a Bucyrus-Erie 1850-B electric shovel, built from 1962 to 1963 and the second largest of its kind working in the 1960s and 1970s. It stripped the earth off shallow coal seams here until 1974, when coal stopped paying. The Pittsburg & Midway Coal Mining Company gave it in 1984 for a mining museum, which opened in 1985.',
  'The shovel itself, standing in the old strip-mine ground with the museum beside it (Wikipedia). Climbing the boom is not allowed.',
  'A coal shovel the size of a building, left where it last dug.',
  'Big Brutus museum, 6509 NW 60th Street, West Mineral; admission charged and camping offered (museum page). Park at the museum.',
  [W('Big Brutus'),['Big Brutus: plan your visit','https://bigbrutus.org/plan-your-visit/']],
  brick='no',hours='Daily 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; 9 a.m. to 4 p.m. November to March'),
N('coronado-heights-castle','Coronado Heights castle','Lindsborg, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',38.61322,-97.70351,'site','public','drive','','1936',
  'Coronado Heights is the southernmost of seven sandstone bluffs northwest of Lindsborg, about 300 feet above the valley; Spanish chain mail found nearby in 1915 led a Bethany College professor to tie it to Coronado\'s expedition. A road up the hill was built in 1920. In 1936 the Works Progress Administration built a stone shelter like a small castle on the summit, with picnic places.',
  'The Dakota sandstone castle on the top, used as a picnic shelter and lookout, with the WPA picnic areas round it (Lindsborg visitors\' page). The park went on the National Register in 2010.',
  'A Depression-era sandstone castle on a bluff named for the Spanish conquistador.',
  'Coronado Heights Park, owned by the Smoky Valley Historical Association (Wikipedia) and open to visitors (Lindsborg visitors\' page gives the drive). From Lindsborg go west on Lincoln, north two miles on Coronado Avenue (13th Avenue), west a mile on Coronado Heights Road, and follow the road to the top. No hours checked.',
  [W('Coronado Heights'),['Visit Lindsborg: Coronado Heights','https://www.visitlindsborg.com/coronado-heights']],
  th=[38.61317,-97.70329],thn='Lot on Coronado Heights Park Road',brick='no'),
N('garden-of-eden-lucas','Garden of Eden','Lucas, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.05784,-98.535,'exact','open','walk','','1905–1932',
  'Samuel P. Dinsmoor, a Union veteran and retired teacher, began in 1905 to build a twelve-room "log cabin" whose logs are limestone quarried near Wilson Lake, and spent the rest of his life filling the lot with more than 200 concrete sculptures of his Populist and religious ideas. He died in 1932 and lies in a concrete coffin with a glass lid in the mausoleum on the lot.',
  'The limestone-log house and the concrete figures, among them "Labor Crucified", and the mausoleum, where a tour shows his body (Wikipedia). The sculptures can be seen from the street at any time; the house and mausoleum by guided tour.',
  'A Civil War veteran\'s concrete sermon on politics, with himself on view in the tomb.',
  'Garden of Eden, 2nd Street and Kansas Avenue, Lucas. Tour hours from the Garden of Eden\'s page; owner not checked. Park on the street.',
  [W('Samuel P. Dinsmoor'),['Garden of Eden, Lucas','https://gardenofedenlucas.org/']],
  brick='no',hours='Guided tours May to October daily 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.; March and April daily 11 to 4; November to February Thursday to Sunday 1 to 4'),
N('mine-creek-battlefield','Mine Creek Battlefield','Pleasanton, Kansas','ks','fort','traces',38.14504,-94.72341,'area','public','walk','','1864',
  'On October 25, 1864, two Union brigades attacked and beat a much larger Confederate force of Sterling Price\'s army on the banks of Mine Creek, one of the largest cavalry battles of the Civil War and the only major battle in Kansas. The state bought 160 acres in 1970 and 120 more in 1974; a visitor center opened in 1998.',
  'Walking trails over the battlefield along Mine Creek, with the visitor center (Wikipedia; Kansas Historical Society). The numbers engaged differ: about 2,800 Union against 8,000 Confederates in Wikipedia, about 2,500 against 7,000 on the society\'s page.',
  'The one big Civil War battle on Kansas soil, fought by cavalry at a creek crossing.',
  'Mine Creek Civil War Battlefield State Historic Site, Kansas Historical Society, 20485 K-52, half a mile west of US 69 at the Mound City exit. The grounds are a self-guided walk-through site open dawn to dusk all year (society page). Park in the visitor center lot off K-52; the trails run south from the visitor center toward the creek.',
  [W('Mine Creek Battlefield State Historic Site'),['Kansas Historical Society: Mine Creek',KS+'mine_creek']],
  th=[38.14532,-94.72400],thn='Visitor center lot, K-52',conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='Grounds dawn to dusk; visitor center Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., Sunday 1 to 5, mid-April to early October (shut until April 2027)'),
N('fort-harker-kanopolis','Fort Harker guardhouse and officers\' quarters','Kanopolis, Kansas','ks','fort','standing',38.71035,-98.16141,'exact','open','walk','forts','1866–1872',
  'Fort Harker, which replaced the nearby Fort Ellsworth, was an Army post from November 1866 to October 1872 and one of the main supply points for the posts farther west; the town of Kanopolis now stands on it. The west end of its parade ground was a two-story stone guardhouse with six cells upstairs, and three of the officers\' quarters were red sandstone.',
  'The Ellsworth County Historical Society keeps three of the fort\'s buildings as a museum: the stone guardhouse, the commanding officer\'s quarters and a junior officers\' quarters, with a train depot and later exhibits. A second junior officers\' quarters is a private house (Wikipedia; Kansapedia).',
  'A frontier fort\'s jail, still standing in a small town\'s streets.',
  'Fort Harker Museum, Ellsworth County Historical Society, on Ohio Avenue at Colorado Street. Hours not checked: no page of the society\'s own was found. The private officers\' quarters is a home: look from the street.',
  [W('Fort Harker (Kansas)'),['Kansapedia: Fort Harker',KS+'kansapedia/fort-harker/11802']],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
N('cottonwood-ranch-studley','Cottonwood Ranch','Studley, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.35556,-100.17361,'site','public','drive','','1885',
  'Abraham Pratt, an Englishman, settled here with his sons John Fenton and Tom; from 1885 they built a one-room stone house with a sod roof and dirt floor, later roofed with wood and enlarged. The ranch had a sod stable, a sod-walled corral and other buildings, and became a state historic site in 1982.',
  'The stone ranch house and the ranch grounds (Wikipedia). The exterior is open all year; the interior\'s opening is "to be determined" (state page).',
  'An English family\'s stone ranch on the High Plains.',
  'Cottonwood Ranch State Historic Site, Kansas Historical Society, near Studley. The society\'s page: exterior open year-round dawn to dusk, interior opening to be determined, admission free; call ahead for the schedule. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Cottonwood Ranch'),['Kansas Historical Society: Cottonwood Ranch, plan your visit',KS+'p/cottonwood-ranch-plan-your-visit/15528']],
  brick='no',hours='Grounds dawn to dusk all year; interior opening to be determined'),
N('goodnow-house-manhattan','Goodnow House','Manhattan, Kansas','ks','landmark','standing',39.19281,-96.5956,'exact','open','walk','','1857',
  'A two-story stone house built in 1857, where Isaac Goodnow, an abolitionist and a founder of both Manhattan and Kansas State University, lived with his wife Ellen from 1861. A friend who inherited it gave it with many of the Goodnows\' belongings to the Kansas Historical Society, which runs it.',
  'Rough limestone walls with smooth quoins at the corners, gabled roofs over the main block and a one-story wing, later additions, and a cellar under the 1857 part (Wikipedia). Inside, the Goodnows\' belongings.',
  'A free-state founder\'s stone house, kept with his own furniture.',
  'Goodnow House State Historic Site, Kansas Historical Society, 2301 Claflin Road, Manhattan. Hours from the society\'s page; parking not checked.',
  [W('Goodnow House'),['Kansas Historical Society: Goodnow House, plan your visit',KS+'p/goodnow-house-plan-your-visit/15854']],
  brick='no',hours='Tuesday to Friday 2 to 5 p.m.'),
]
